# e-portfolio-2-Ethical-Theory

A collection of artefacts that demonstrate what I have learnt about Ethical Theory this week.

Artefact 1: Scholarly Article

Ethical Decision-Making in Artificial Intelligence: A Logic Programming Approach.Ethical Decision-Making in Artificial Intelligence: A Logic Programming Approach.


A summary of the artefact.

The four ethics strategies that used most in computational ethics are reviewed by Machado et al. (2024, p. 2710): utilitarianism, deontology, virtue ethics and ethical pluralism. Utilitarianism is a theory that determines if an action leads to greatest happiness overall, deontology is a theory that decides if an action is based on a moral rule or obligation, no matter what the outcome, and virtue ethics is a theory that decides if an action is based on traits of character like fairness and honesty. The authors then suggest a logic-programming approach that allows an AI system to use strict rules and flexible rules concurrently, so that, for example, a self-driving car could use strict rules to follow traffic law while flexible rules to avoid an accident (Machado et al. 2024, p. 2714).

Justification on why I chose the artefact

This article was selected because it was a comparison of Kantianism and utilitarianism, the two philosophies that received the most attention in the Week 4 workshop, and the strengths and shortcomings of these theories when they are implemented in actual software. Prior to reading it, I thought that an AI system would simply be given one “right” theory. I was surprised to hear that one of the reasons designers combine the two types of reasoning is that neither one can deal well with all situations, given that the theme of the workshop was that Kantianism fails when duties clash. It was also provided a real-life example in healthcare and autonomous vehicles, allowing the theory to become more tangible and relevant to my future work as an ICT professional.


Artefact 2: Blog Article

Ethics and dilemmas with self-driving cars, or the 21st Century Trolley Problem.


Write a brief summary of the artefact.

ZH (2026) uses three moral theories to analyze a self-driving car situation of a truck that is emptying its load and the car has to choose between hitting a family car, a motorcyclist, or a truck. The "right" action from a utilitarian perspective is the one that causes the least overall harm, meaning that the car would have to strike the motorcyclist since one life is worth more than two or four. If the car should not take any action, it would be more dangerous, then, from a Kantian-deontological perspective, it should not even try to cause harm. The real fault in the article is that it places the blame on a virtue ethics perspective on the company that made the car, as its "decisions" are based on the values that were put into the car and not on the car's own character.

Justification on why I chose the artefact

This artefact was particularly interesting as it is the same three theories covered in the workshop and demonstrates how each one provides a truly different answer to the same ICT scenario. I liked how the author acknowledged the utilitarian response "doesn't feel right," even though it is a logical response, and how the workshop discussed that our natural sense of duty may get left behind in utilitarianism. I realized that selecting an ethical theory is not a purely academic endeavor, but a design choice that autonomous vehicle engineers are making, that has real-world consequences for whom they are causing harm on the road.


Artefact 3: News Article

AI technology is being misused to cheat on students at universities around the world.The use of AI technology to cheat on students is being misused in universities all over the world.


This is a summary of the artefact.

Australian Catholic University has been accused of using the Turnitin AI detection tool to deceive students – including a final year nursing student named Madeleine – with evidence of their using generative AI. But when Madeleine shared her results after six months, the accusation was dropped and she says she missed out on a graduate nursing position. The university had almost 6,000 alleged cheating cases in 2024, around 90 per cent linked to AI, yet Turnitin itself warns its detector should never be the sole basis for a misconduct finding. 1/4 of all referrals were finally rejected on investigation.

Justification on why I chose the artefact

I selected this article because it is nearly a reflection of the GenAI scenario we explored during our Week 4 workshop, where a student was assessed on a work created using their ChatGPT. Here, it is not the student's action that needs testing as per the ethical theory of the student, but it is the action of the University. The Categorical Imperative's second formulation would say that, if a University punishes a student because of the use of an unreliable instrument of which it has insufficient evidence, it is treating the student as a means to its own end, namely academic reputation, instead of as its end. The article also ties in with the social contract theory because students can be relied upon by this institution to make sure it has fair process, and in exchange it expects students to follow its rules – and that trust was violated.
